1
00:00:00,103 --> 00:00:04,900
Short series, weak patterns or missing references can prevent dating.

2
00:00:05,355 --> 00:00:08,671
Dated members may not represent the whole structure.

3
00:00:09,132 --> 00:00:13,434
State what remains undated and propose further observations.

4
00:00:13,895 --> 00:00:17,250
Unsuccessful dating has causes worth recording.

5
00:00:17,711 --> 00:00:23,712
Reporting only successful samples can give a misleading picture of the available material.

6
00:00:24,515 --> 00:00:29,523
Specimen ID, location, species and outer-edge preservation.

7
00:00:29,978 --> 00:00:34,550
Reference, overlap, candidate dates, mismatches and checks.

8
00:00:35,004 --> 00:00:39,511
Type of conclusion, assumptions, alternatives and references.

9
00:00:39,965 --> 00:00:44,333
Explain the path from sample location through the outer-ring result to felling or

10
00:00:44,346 --> 00:00:48,172
construction inference so a reader can evaluate it.

11
00:00:48,975 --> 00:00:53,858
Does heartwood ending in 1580 establish construction in 1580?.

12
00:00:54,161 --> 00:00:58,925
Where do you add a total sapwood estimate if five rings already survive?.

13
00:00:59,227 --> 00:01:03,135
Give a reason and one useful additional observation for each.

14
00:01:03,596 --> 00:01:09,017
Distinguish the outer-ring date, felling and construction, and identify the further evidence

15
00:01:09,030 --> 00:01:11,432
needed for each inference.

16
00:01:12,235 --> 00:01:15,407
Lesson 6 introduces dendroecology.

17
00:01:15,869 --> 00:01:20,217
Study suppression, release, disturbance and regeneration.

18
00:01:20,671 --> 00:01:23,816
Keep distinguishing tree dates from event dates.

19
00:01:24,277 --> 00:01:29,724
Next we examine forest history, again combining different kinds of evidence to interpret

20
00:01:29,737 --> 00:01:32,964
human activity and tree responses.
